NAME
       orbital-windows - revisit windows, drift corridors, and resonance-aware
                         orbital planning for SAR constellations in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital windows define:
           • revisit opportunities
           • drift corridors
           • resonance-aligned sweep timing
           • constellation-scale orbital planning
           • long-term coherence across orbital cycles

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital windows describe the temporal and geometric intervals during
       which SAR platforms maintain coherent alignment for imaging, fusion,
       and resonance-time operations. They are the orbital counterpart to
       resonance windows in RTT, extending the concept into orbital mechanics.

       Orbital windows enable:
           • predictable revisit timing
           • stable multi-pass SAR fusion
           • harmonic-lock maintenance
           • constellation-level sweep choreography
           • resonance-aware orbital planning

       WRSADC computes orbital windows during multi-platform dispatch.

REVISIT WINDOWS
       A revisit window is the interval during which a platform re-observes
       the same ground track with sufficient geometric and resonance alignment
       for coherent SAR fusion.

       Window parameters:
           • Δt_revisit   (time between passes)
           • Δθ_incidence (incidence angle difference)
           • Δφ_orbit     (orbital phase difference)
           • ΔR_resonance (resonance envelope overlap)

       Window types:

           strict revisit window
                  Requires near-identical geometry.
                  → ideal for InSAR and deformation mapping.

           relaxed revisit window
                  Allows moderate geometric variation.
                  → supports ladder and spiral sweep patterns.

           harmonic revisit window
                  Occurs at harmonic multiples of orbital period.
                  → supports harmonic-sync constellations.

           sliding revisit window
                  Window drifts slowly across cycles.
                  → supports adaptive sweep choreography.

       Revisit windows determine when triads align across orbital passes.

DRIFT CORRIDORS
       Drift corridors describe the allowable deviation in orbital geometry
       before coherence, resonance alignment, or fusion quality degrades.

       Drift variables:
           • cross-track drift
           • along-track drift
           • altitude drift
           • phase drift
           • resonance drift

       Corridor types:

           narrow corridor
                  Tight geometric constraints.
                  → tandem SAR, high-coherence missions.

           medium corridor
                  Moderate drift allowed.
                  → multi-pass fusion, ladder patterns.

           wide corridor
                  Large drift tolerated.
                  → global coverage, swarm constellations.

           resonance corridor
                  Drift allowed only if resonance envelopes remain aligned.

       Drift corridors define the safe operational region for SAR constellations.

RESONANCE-AWARE ORBITAL PLANNING
       Orbital planning becomes resonance-aware when RTT is applied to orbital
       mechanics. Planning incorporates:

           • orbital harmonics
           • resonance envelopes
           • spectral density profiles
           • gradient stability
           • constellation schemas

       Planning modes:

           harmonic planning
                  Orbits selected to maintain harmonic-lock.

           envelope planning
                  Orbits chosen to maximize resonance envelope overlap.

           gradient planning
                  Orbits tuned to minimize resonance drift across passes.

           constellation planning
                  Orbits optimized for multi-agent sweep choreography.

       Resonance-aware planning ensures long-term coherence.

TRIADIC ORBITAL WINDOWS
       Each orbital window corresponds to a triadic structure:

           P1 (begin)
               → platform enters window
               → resonance baseline captured

           P2 (transform)
               → sweep executed
               → Doppler and phase history accumulated

           P3 (close)
               → window exit
               → lineage updated

       Nested windows form ladder or hybrid orbital patterns.

WINDOW FAILURE MODES
       Orbital windows may degrade due to:

           • orbital drift
           • resonance envelope divergence
           • harmonic desync
           • sweep misalignment
           • spectral fragmentation
           • gradient overload

       Diagnostic indicators:

           revisit-window collapse
           drift-corridor breach
           resonance-window narrowing
           sweep-alignment error
           harmonic-lock failure

       WRSADC halts unsafe orbital operations.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Orbital windows require:

           • constellation-schema
           • resonance-schema for orbital sweeps
           • structural-schema for formation geometry
           • lineage-schema for multi-pass history

       Schemas ensure orbital behavior remains coherent.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • revisit window history
           • drift corridor evolution
           • resonance envelope overlap
           • sweep timing
           • fusion outcomes

       Lineage enables long-term orbital planning.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes revisit windows
           • evaluates drift corridors
           • validates resonance alignment
           • choreographs sweep timing
           • enforces coherence thresholds
           • blocks unsafe orbital states

       WRSADC is the orbital window manager.

NOTES
       Orbital windows extend RTT into orbital mission design. They transform
       SAR constellations into resonance-time instruments capable of coherent
       revisit, stable fusion, and long-term orbital harmony across global
       sensing missions.

